+# Keep post revisions on every edit
+
+## Context
+
+Saving the edit form overwrote a post for good, and authors lost work to
+accidental overwrites. They need a history of earlier versions and a way to
+put one back.
+
+## Decision
+
+- Every write to a post's title, excerpt or body goes through `Post.revise()`,
+  which copies the stored content into `post_revisions` and then updates the
+  post, in one transaction. The post is re-read inside the transaction, so
+  concurrent saves each keep what the other wrote.
+- Restoring a revision is an edit like any other: it calls `Post.revise()`, so
+  the version it replaces is kept and the restored revision stays in the list.
+- A save that changes nothing still keeps a revision. One rule ("each
+  successful save adds one revision") is easier to reason about than
+  comparing content, at the cost of an occasional duplicate entry.
+- History is authorized with `PostPolicy.update`: whoever may edit a post may
+  see and restore its revisions. There is no separate ability.
+- A restore looks the revision up by id *and* post id, so a revision of
+  another post answers 404 even for the same author.
+- `Post.purge()` deletes a post's revisions explicitly before the post. The
+  foreign key also declares `ON DELETE CASCADE`, but SQLite only honours it
+  with `foreign_keys` enabled, which the app does not depend on.
+
+## Consequences
+
+- Code that updates a post's content with `Post.update()` directly bypasses
+  the history; use `Post.revise()`.
+- Revisions are never pruned, so the table grows with every save. Pruning and
+  side-by-side comparison are out of scope for now.
